 <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">

 <aside class="mb-10 border border-hairline bg-panel p-7">
 <p class="mb-3 font-mono text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">Quick Answer</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal sm:text-xl speakable-answer">Employers may use approved apparel identifiers for apprentices and journeymen. Titles, year levels, colors, and hard-hat rules vary. Confirm each detail with the employer, site, apprenticeship program, and exact product manufacturer.</p>
 </aside>

 <h2 id="why-differentiate" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Why Apprentice/Journeyman Differentiation Exists</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">A visual identifier is optional and must never substitute for verified qualification, supervision, training records, access control, or the employer's safety program.</p>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Identification.</strong> An employer may use an approved identifier only as a communication aid under its written policy.</li>
 <li><strong>Administration.</strong> Task assignment and supervision must use verified records and qualified leadership, not apparel markings.</li>
 <li><strong>Recognition.</strong> Training, credentialing, and role requirements vary by trade, jurisdiction, employer, and program. Any visual system should reflect the employer's verified classifications and policy.</li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="union-programs" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Union Apprenticeship Programs in Vegas</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Training and apparel rules vary and change. Obtain the current written program guidance directly from the relevant organization before specifying any identifier or garment:</p>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong><a href="https://ibew357.org/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600" rel="noopener">IBEW Local 357</a></strong>: request current program and apparel guidance directly.</li>
 <li><strong><a href="https://www.ualocal525.org/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600" rel="noopener">UA Local 525</a></strong>: request current program and apparel guidance directly.</li>
 <li><strong>Other apprenticeship programs:</strong> confirm current requirements directly with the program and employer.</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Do not assume a local requires a specific shirt, title, color, or identifier. Obtain the current handbook or written program guidance before building the apparel specification.</p>

 <h2 id="differentiation-patterns" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Three Standard Differentiation Patterns</h2>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">1. Year-Level Embroidery</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">An employer may consider an approved text label, but it must match verified records and current policy. It cannot establish qualification, authorize work, or replace supervision.</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">2. Sleeve Stripe System</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">An employer may consider an approved visual system if its written policy defines and maintains it. The identifier cannot establish qualification or authorize work.</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">3. Color Hierarchy</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">An employer may consider a shirt-color hierarchy if the program approves it. Hard-hat colors remain subject to employer and site policy, exact product selection, and current manufacturer instructions.</p>

 <h2 id="merit-shops" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Merit Shop Adaptations</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Non-union shops use the same logic with different titles. Common Vegas service-trade tier programs:</p>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Plumbing/HVAC service:</strong> Helper → Tech 1 → Tech 2 → Lead Tech → Master Tech</li>
 <li><strong>Electrical service:</strong> Apprentice → Journeyman → Master → Foreman</li>
 <li><strong>Roofing crews:</strong> Helper → Roofer → Lead Roofer → Foreman</li>
 <li><strong>Concrete:</strong> Laborer → Finisher → Lead Finisher → Foreman</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">If an employer uses tier labels, they must match verified personnel records and current policy. A shirt does not prove a credential, license, qualification, or authority.</p>

 <h2 id="fr-apprentices" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">FR-Rated Apprentice Apparel</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">The employer's electrical hazard assessment determines whether arc-rated apparel is required and which rating applies. Any identifier requires current written manufacturer approval for the exact garment, thread, backing, method, and placement.</p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Welding apparel also depends on the employer's hazard assessment and task. Do not use a universal layering or decoration rule. Confirm the exact products and manufacturer instructions.</p>

